  ],
  "text": "Your blood vessels, laid end to end, would wrap around the Earth twice. Pipework that long would normally need an engine room and a crew to keep it running. Yours is driven by something the size of your fist, beating a hundred thousand times a day. It pushes seven and a half thousand litres through you every single day. There is enough pressure behind it to send a jet of blood several metres into the air. A single blood cell leaves your heart and is back again in under a minute. And here is the part that unsettles people. It does not take orders from your brain. It has its own electrical pacemaker, a patch of cells that fires all by itself. Take a heart out of a body, give it oxygen, and it keeps beating on the table. Two and a half billion beats, and not one of them was your decision. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
